Three different sets of terms apply to three different kinds of thing in this
repository. The distinction is not cosmetic: this project exists to be able to say
where a word list came from, and a licence that overclaimed would undo that.

APACHE-2.0 — the code and the documentation

  build.mjs, sources.mjs, harvest.mjs, conform.mjs, saturation.mjs, weed.mjs,
  check-dumps.mjs, collections.mjs, verify.mjs, and the Markdown that explains them.

CC0-1.0 — the generated word list and the evidence behind it

  words.txt
  ATTESTATIONS.tsv
  status.json, SATURATION.md, COLLECTIONS.md, searched.tsv

  These are Blinkered's own compilation and its working. A word ships because three
  independent collections of text were found to contain it, and the record of which
  collections and where in them is a statement of fact about those texts rather than a
  copy of them. Nothing a licence governs was taken from the dictionaries that proposed
  the candidates, which is the whole point of the method. To the extent any right
  subsists in the compilation, it is waived: CC0 1.0 Universal.
  https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/

NOT OURS TO LICENSE — the words we could not prove

  dropped.tsv

  Every other file here rests on evidence we gathered. This one does not: it is the
  candidates that failed, and a candidate that failed is a word we have nothing to say
  about except that somebody's dictionary proposed it. That makes the file a subset of
  that dictionary, and it carries that dictionary's terms rather than ours:

      CC-BY-SA-4.0

  See blinkered-attestation/candidates/ja/LICENSE and PROVENANCE.md for what was
  relied on and why. Redistributing dropped.tsv must satisfy those terms. Neither
  Apache-2.0 nor CC0 above applies to it, and neither can be made to apply to it by us.
